#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""facilitator: board CLI.

  facilitator run [--attach] [--spawn] [--force] [--dry-run]
      start the server and the app window. That is all it touches by default:
      it says the board is up and nothing more. The board listens on the port
      in run.config.json and the one above it; when something else holds that
      pair, run moves the board to the next free pair, writes the new port
      into run.config.json, points the phone's Tailscale link at it when that
      link was this board's own, and says so in one line. The board stays on
      that pair until it is taken too. Once a phone password has been set
      (facilitator password set), run also leaves the phone bridge on, with
      the checks `facilitator bridge on` makes, and says nothing when it does
      or when it already was; when it cannot (Tailscale not connected, say) it
      prints one line saying the phone link is off and why, and the board is
      up all the same. With no password set it does nothing about the bridge.
      --attach types the attach
      instruction into the lane's existing tmux claude (send-keys), and
      --spawn additionally creates a fresh session
      for lanes with no reachable one. --force ignores the lane-covered guard.
      --dry-run names the bridge step too.
  facilitator restart [--dry-run]
      stop this board's server and start it again on the same port. One
      process is stopped: the one holding the listening socket, read back as
      this folder's server.py first, by SIGTERM and never by SIGKILL, and the
      port is polled until it is really free before the replacement starts.
      The browser is read, never driven: a window or tab already showing the
      board is left exactly as it is and a line asks for a reload, and a new
      chromeless app window is opened only when nothing is showing the board.
      When the browser cannot be read nothing is opened and nothing is said.
      Touches no lane, no tmux session and no bridge, and never moves the
      board to another port. --dry-run says what it would do and does nothing.
  facilitator stop [--dry-run] [--force]
      end this board's server and close the board's own Chrome window. The
      server is found and stopped the way restart does it: the one process
      holding the listening socket, read back as this folder's server.py
      first, asked to quit with SIGTERM, and both of its ports polled until
      they are really free. Only then does Chrome come into it: a window is
      closed only when every tab in it is the board's own page, by its window
      id and after its tabs are read again, and a board tab sharing a window
      with other tabs is left open and said so. Chrome is never started, and
      when it cannot be asked one line says so. A server still there after
      10 seconds is left alone and reported; --force then ends it outright
      with one SIGKILL to that same process, read a third time first. Once
      the server is gone (or was not running), and a phone password has been
      set, the phone bridge is taken off as `facilitator bridge off` does it:
      only this board's root handler goes, every other Serve path, port,
      Funnel route and Service stays. It says nothing when the bridge is off
      already or goes off, and one line when it cannot be taken off; a stop
      that leaves the server running leaves the bridge as it is. With no
      password set it only notes an active bridge. Touches no lane, no tmux
      session and no saved file. --dry-run says what it would do and does
      nothing.
  facilitator status
      one-line board state
  facilitator magic CARD KIND (CONTENT | --file PATH | -) [--title TITLE]
  facilitator magic CARD close|reopen|show
      fill a card's magic box, the box beside the card on the Mac
      board and above it on the phone, and open it in place of what it held,
      so an agent can show the owner what words carry badly without being
      asked. It waits on its card and never takes the keys. KIND is text
      (shown as typed), markdown, html (a page of its own, run sealed off
      with no network), image (a picture file, or one https address the
      board downloads once) or url (one https website: shown live when it
      allows that, as a square holding the site's name, which opens it in a
      new page, when it does not). The box shows the content beside a short
      bar whose cross lets the owner close it; --title is kept with it.
      CONTENT is
      the words or the address; --file sends a file's contents and - reads
      standard input. close hides the box and keeps it, reopen shows it
      again, show prints what the board says it holds. Prints the board's
      answer as one JSON line; exits 1 with the board's own words when it
      refuses, and with the status line when the board is down.
  facilitator remote pair [--name NAME]
  facilitator remote list
  facilitator remote remove NAME
      connect one of the owner's own servers to this board, so an agent
      running there works a project's cards like one on this Mac. Nothing
      here logs in to a server: the server connects out to the running
      board's agent door, a listener on this Mac's tailnet addresses alone.
      pair asks the board for a pairing code (good once, for 10 minutes) and
      prints the one line the owner runs on the server, logged in there by
      hand, and the code it asks for: that puts the skill, the runbook and
      the docs in ~/.facilitator on the server, trades the code for the
      server's own key and starts the server's link, which keeps connecting
      out to the board from then on. --name names the server; otherwise it
      takes the server's host name, and pairing a name again pairs that
      server afresh. The server's folders are then on the board's folder
      picker. list shows each server and whether its link is connected;
      remove forgets a server on this Mac, with its projects' keys, and
      leaves the server's own files for the owner to delete. add is retired:
      the board no longer logs in to servers.
  facilitator bridge [on|off] [--dry-run]
      switch the persistent phone bridge on or off. With no on/off word it
      toggles the current Tailscale Serve state. Turning it on prints a QR
      code that opens the phone page (/m); turning it off removes only this
      board's root handler and leaves unrelated Serve configuration alone.
      The server keeps listening on 127.0.0.1 only; Tailscale does the
      proxying to a password-gated listener. A saved phone session lasts until
      sign-out. Set or reset the app password with `facilitator password set`.
      Needs HTTPS certificates switched on once for the tailnet, in the
      Tailscale admin console; says so and stops when they are off. run and
      stop make the same change themselves once a password is set, so this
      command is for switching the link by hand and for the QR code. When run
      moved the board and could not move the link with it, the config keeps
      the old port as moved_from: on points that old rule at the board's
      pair, and off takes it away.
      --dry-run prints the commands instead of running them
  facilitator bridge setup
      set the phone link up after saying no to it at install, or check it
      again. Prints the installer's banner, then only its Mobile app step,
      the same questions in the same words: Tailscale, its HTTPS
      certificates, and the app password (an existing one is kept). Run
      without a terminal it asks nothing and says so. It does not switch
      the link on; `facilitator run` does once a password is set, or
      `facilitator bridge on`.
  facilitator uninstall [--keep-attachments | --remove-attachments]
      remove what install made: the .venv, app/tests/node_modules and the
      generated config files, owned shell command, owned agent-skill links, and
      any Claude Code status line entry an older install added. Keeps the
      board's data in data/ (state.json, transcript.jsonl, settings.json, logs,
      bridge credentials, vapid-key.pem and the backups).
      After its banner it asks whether to keep the card
      attachments; Enter, an unclear answer or no terminal keeps them, and the
      two flags answer without asking.
      Never removes this folder or stops a running server.
  facilitator password set
      prompt twice for the phone bridge password; replacing it signs out every
      phone immediately. Passwords are never accepted on the command line.
      Runs on the .venv's Python 3.14, since the python3 macOS ships cannot
      make the password's hash; with no working .venv it says to run
      ./install.sh first.
  facilitator update
      bring this checkout up to date without running git yourself. Stops and
      changes nothing when the checkout has uncommitted changes. Pulls the
      current branch's upstream forward only (git pull --ff-only: never a merge
      or a rewrite), then hands over to the freshly pulled code. On a checkout
      from before data/, that code first moves the board's files into data/,
      once: everything is backed up, copied and checked, and the old copies
      are removed only after the board shows the same cards from data/. It
      then syncs the .venv to requirements.txt and runs the numbered steps in
      app/updates/ that have not run here yet, each once. A .venv that is
      missing, broken or not on Python 3.14 is first built again on the
      Python 3.14 uv manages; a board running on the old one is stopped before
      that and started again after. Restarts the board only when it is running
      and the pull changed the board's own code (its Python files or
      requirements) or update steps ran, or when the move or the rebuild
      stopped it; when only pages changed, the board keeps running and a
      reload shows them. The Mac board starts this same command when the
      person says yes to its update pop-up.
      Ends with the old and new version.

The program lives in app/ inside the checkout, and everything the board writes
lives in data/ beside it, which git ignores. Reads data/run.config.json:
machine-local, since lanes name real directories and tmux sessions;
./install.sh writes it from app/run.config.example.json for you to fill in.
This script is Python stdlib only and runs on the python3 macOS ships (3.9 or
newer); ./install.sh builds the server's data/.venv on a Python 3.14 that uv
manages, from app/requirements.txt. run starts the server with that .venv's
python when the folder is there, and with its own interpreter otherwise.
password set always runs on that .venv's python; the commands that repair or
remove .venv (update, uninstall) never do. The facilitator file at the top of
the checkout only hands over to this one.
"""

